# Blood Donation Network API

## 1. Overview

The API is the **source of truth** for the entire application. The frontend only displays what the API returns and never duplicates business rules.

The backend decides:

- which donor blood types are compatible with a blood request (R-1, R-2)
- whether a donor is in their 56-day post-donation rest period and their exact `nextAllowedDonationDate` (R-3, R-4, R-5)
- whether a donor is eligible for a request (`ELIGIBLE`, `REST_PERIOD`, `UNAVAILABLE`, `INACTIVE`) and the exact rejection reason when they are not (R-6)
- the ranked order of eligible donors and the excluded-donor summary counts (`restPeriod`, `unavailable`, `inactive`) for any open request (R-7, R-8)
- the request lifecycle status (`OPEN`, `FULFILLED`, `CANCELLED`) and `unitsStillNeeded` (`unitsRequired - unitsCollected`) (R-9, R-10, R-11, R-14)
- whether a pledge is accepted or rejected (at most one active `PLEDGED` pledge per donor, and active pledges on a request never exceed `unitsStillNeeded`) (R-15, R-16, R-17)
- atomic recording of a completed donation inside a single database transaction (`prisma.$transaction`), automatically updating `unitsCollected`, completing the donor's pledge, starting the donor's 56-day rest period, and—when `unitsCollected === unitsRequired`—marking the request `FULFILLED` and releasing all remaining `PLEDGED` pledges to `RELEASED` (R-18, R-19, R-20, R-21, R-22)
- privacy filtering on public endpoints so donor personal data, patient references, and coordinator notes are never exposed to the public (R-23, R-24)

The API uses **Node.js**, **NestJS**, **TypeScript**, **PostgreSQL** through **Prisma ORM**, **JWT authentication**, and **Swagger / OpenAPI**. All data persists across server restarts.

